# ============================================================
# NPM Security Hardening — Supply Chain Attack Mitigation
# ============================================================
# Background: axios@1.14.1 / 0.30.4 compromise (2026-03-31)
# - Attacker hijacked maintainer npm account
# - Injected hidden dependency with postinstall RAT dropper
# - Self-destructing payload left no forensic trace
#
# This config neutralizes that entire class of attack.
# ============================================================

# LAYER 1: Kill postinstall scripts globally.
# This is the single most effective defense against npm supply
# chain attacks. The axios RAT, event-stream backdoor, and
# ua-parser-js cryptominer ALL relied on postinstall hooks.
# If a future dependency needs native compilation, use:
#   npm rebuild <package-name>
ignore-scripts=true

# LAYER 2: Require provenance attestation for packages.
# Packages published with npm's OIDC Trusted Publisher mechanism
# include cryptographic proof of their CI origin. The malicious
# axios@1.14.1 was manually published without OIDC binding —
# this setting would flag that discrepancy.
# Note: Not all packages have provenance yet, so this is
# advisory rather than blocking.
# See: https://docs.npmjs.com/generating-provenance-statements
